      "text": "To find the probability that the first card drawn is a 6 and the second card drawn is a Queen from a standard deck of 52 cards, we can break down the problem into two steps:\n\n1. Calculate the probability of drawing a 6 as the first card.\n2. Calculate the probability of drawing a Queen as the second card, given that the first card drawn was a 6.\n\nThe deck has 52 cards in total. There are 4 sixes in a deck (one for each suit: hearts, clubs, spades, and diamonds). So, the probability of drawing a 6 as the first card is:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{First card is a 6}) = \\frac{4}{52} = \\frac{1}{13}\n\\]\n\nAfter drawing a 6, there are now 51 cards left in the deck, and there are still 4 Queens in the deck. So, the probability of drawing a Queen as the second card, given that the first card drawn was a 6, is:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{Second card is a Queen} \\mid \\text{First card is a 6}) = \\frac{4}{51}\n\\]\n\nThe probability of both events happening (first card is a 6 and second card is a Queen) is the product of the probabilities of each individual event:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{First card is a 6 and second card is a Queen}) = P(\\text{First card is a 6}) \\times P(\\text{Second card is a Queen} \\mid \\text{First card is a 6}) = \\frac{1}{13} \\times \\frac{4}{51} = \\frac{4}{663}\n\\]\n\nSo, the probability that the first card is a 6 and the second card is a Queen is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{4}{663}}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this using Python code.\n```python\n# Calculate the probability using the formula derived\nfrom fractions import Fraction\n\n# Probability of drawing a 6 first\nprob_first_6 = Fraction(4, 52)\n\n# Probability of drawing a Queen second given that the first card was a 6\nprob_second_queen_given_first_6 = Fraction(4, 51)\n\n# Total probability\ntotal_prob = prob_first_6 * prob_second_queen_given_first_6\nprint(total_prob)\n```",
